# --- Supabase JS client (HTTPS) — PostgREST, Auth, Storage, Realtime ---
# Project Settings → API: Project URL + anon (legacy) or publishable key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="your-anon-or-publishable-key"

# Optional — server-only: invite emails when managers add users (`bulkCreateEmployees`),
# and any future admin Auth API. Never expose in the browser.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="..."

# Public site URL for Supabase invite + password recovery redirects (no trailing slash).
# Production / Vercel: set to your canonical app URL (e.g. https://hr.yourcompany.com).
# If unset, Vercel uses VERCEL_URL; locally defaults to http://localhost:3000.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://your-app.example.com"

# Optional: restrict invite + forgot-password to company domains (comma-separated, no @).
# Example: ALLOWED_AUTH_EMAIL_DOMAINS=yourcompany.com,subsidiary.com
# ALLOWED_AUTH_EMAIL_DOMAINS="yourcompany.com"

# --- Time clock geofence QA bypass (optional) ---
# Comma-separated list of employee emails whose clock-in / clock-out punches
# bypass the geofence radius check. `emily@quicktrackinc.com` is hardcoded as
# a default; use this env var to add more (e.g. for cross-country QA).
# Owner-role accounts ALWAYS bypass; this list is for non-owner QA testers.
# Production deployments should keep this short — it's an explicit security
# trade-off that lets a hardcoded email punch from anywhere.
# GEOFENCE_BYPASS_EMAILS="qa-tester@example.com,another-tester@example.com"

# --- Resend (transactional email) ---
# Used by lib/mail.ts → sendHREmail to fan out approval/denial/publish receipts.
# Leave RESEND_API_KEY empty to disable (no-op + console log) without crashing.
# Get a key: https://resend.com/api-keys
# Set RESEND_FROM to a verified sender ("HR Notifications <hr@your-domain.com>");
# falls back to "HR Notifications <onboarding@resend.dev>" for sandbox testing.
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_xxx"
# RESEND_FROM="HR Notifications <hr@your-domain.com>"

# --- Drizzle / pg (Postgres wire protocol on port 5432) ---
# Still required for migrations, db:push, and server SQL via Drizzle — not replaceable by the URL above.
# Postgres — Supabase: Project Settings → Database → Connection string → URI
#
# NOT the https://xxx.supabase.co project URL — must be postgres:// or postgresql://
#
# Paste whatever Supabase shows as the full URI. Valid shapes include:
#   postgresql://...   or   postgres://...     (same thing for Node/pg)
#   ...@HOST:5432/DATABASE_NAME
#
# The last segment is the database name — on Supabase it is usually "postgres" (default DB).
# If your string has no leading scheme, add: postgresql://
# If your string has no /database at the end, append: /postgres (unless Supabase names it differently)
#
# For drizzle-kit, prefer the DIRECT connection (port 5432), not Transaction pool (6543).
# sslmode / connect_timeout are added automatically in code if missing.
#
# Do NOT keep [YOUR-PASSWORD] or any [ ] brackets — those are docs placeholders only.
# Paste the full URI from Supabase “Copy”, or put the real password with no brackets.
#
# If your password contains @, #, etc., it must be URL-encoded in the URI (@ → %40).
# Or always use Supabase “Copy” on the connection string so encoding is correct.
#
# Optional — if the password has @ or is awkward to embed in the URI:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres@db.YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co:5432/postgres
#   DATABASE_PASSWORD=your raw password (any characters; do not quote unless your tool requires it)
# The app and drizzle-kit merge DATABASE_PASSWORD with encoding via resolveDatabaseUrl().
#
# If you see ECONNREFUSED to an IPv6 address (2600:...), the app prefers IPv4 DNS order;
# if issues persist, try another network or Supabase’s pooler URI from the dashboard.
#
# Example (replace USER, PASSWORD, HOST, DB with your values):
DATABASE_URL="postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DB"

# --- Auth (Supabase) ---
# Enable Email provider: Supabase Dashboard → Authentication → Providers → Email.
# Create a test user: Authentication → Users → Add user (or sign up from /login if sign-ups enabled).
# Run SQL migrations in order through 013 (see supabase/migrations/): 001–008 baseline;
# 009 directory fields; 010 smart groups; 011 employee UPDATE RLS; 012 schedule jobs/groups/shifts;
# 013 shift layers (Connecteam-style metadata).
#
# Dev login (optional): create user in Supabase Auth with:
#   Email:    dev@retailhr.local
#   Password: DevPassword123!
# The login page pre-fills these in `next dev` only.
#
# Auth gate: when OFF, middleware refreshes the session but does not require login (dev/demo).
# If rows fail to load without logging in, run supabase/migrations/004_anon_read_dev_no_auth_gate.sql in the SQL Editor.
#
# --- Production-style gate (recommended before real users) ---
# 1) Require sign-in for all app routes except /login, /auth/callback, /set-password, /forgot-password:
#    NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_ENABLED=true
# 2) In Supabase Dashboard → Authentication → URL configuration, add Redirect URLs:
#    https://YOUR_DOMAIN/auth/callback
#    https://YOUR_DOMAIN/set-password   (some email templates use the redirect target directly)
#    http://localhost:3000/auth/callback   (local dev)
#    http://localhost:3000/set-password
# 3) Set NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL to your production origin so invite/recovery links hit the right host.
# 4) Set SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY on the server (Vercel env) so “Add users” can call inviteUserByEmail.
# 5) Enforce permissions in server actions (users must exist in `employees` with matching email):
#    RBAC_ENABLED=true
# 6) Review Supabase RLS for your org (location-scoped policies in migrations 035, 038, etc.;
#    remove or narrow broad `anon` policies from early migrations when no longer needed).
#
# Public self-service sign-up is disabled in the app (/sign-up → /login). Only managers with
# users.manage can add directory rows; invite emails create Auth accounts.
#
# Optional: force dashboard to use only mock data (no Supabase queries on the home page).
# When true, KPIs show fixed demo totals (e.g. ~318 employees for “All locations”) — turn OFF for real DB/MVP.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_MOCK_DATA=true
#
# Role-based access (server-side): map `employees.role` → permissions in `lib/rbac/matrix.ts`.
# Requires a signed-in user whose email matches `employees.email`. When enabled, unauthenticated
# visits to the app shell redirect to /login.
#   RBAC_ENABLED=true
#
# --- MVP demo (stakeholder walkthrough) ---
# Recommended for demos: leave RBAC_ENABLED unset, use real Supabase data, migrations 001→013.
# Optional: show a dashboard ribbon so recordings don’t look like “prod”.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MVP_DEMO=true
# Or run: npm run dev:demo
#
# See DEMO.md for a short checklist.

# --- Scheduled PTO automation (server-only) ---
# Protects GET /api/cron/pto-automation (wire Vercel Cron or external scheduler daily).
# CRON_SECRET="your-long-random-secret"

# --- QuickTrack Hub SSO (server-only) ---
# Same value as Hub SSO_SHARED_SECRET. Required for /api/auth/sso/consume.
# SSO_SHARED_SECRET="your-long-random-secret"
#
# Required when SSO is enabled — mints Supabase sessions after JWT verify.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="..."
#
# Public Hub origin — after HR sign-out, staff return to Hub login (SSO pilot).
# Production: https://quicktrackhub.vercel.app
# Local Hub: http://localhost:3000 or :3001 (match your npm run dev port)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HUB_APP_URL="https://quicktrackhub.vercel.app"

# --- QuickTrack Hub internal assistant (server-only) ---
# Hub calls HR at /api/internal/assistant/* with header x-internal-api-key.
# Generate a long random string; set the same value on Hub as HR_APP_API_KEY (name TBD by platform).
# HR_INTERNAL_ASSISTANT_API_KEY="your-long-random-secret"
# HR_ASSISTANT_CONNECTOR_ENABLED=true
# Docs: docs/HR-HUB-CONNECTOR-SPEC-v1.md
